mantle rm315
state ready
dialect plain
accent broad light
tier standard
written_for cty_millicent_pitchby
title the proverb in the folded letter
fits stage=adult|old
prefers job=none
traits temper=even talk=quiet warmth=reserved humour=dry honesty=sly piety=ordinary drive=steady
quirk fits_old_sayings_to_the_width_of_a_folded_letter
likes barley_soup dancing sharp_knife autumn_wind
dislikes deep_mud wet_floors
fears a_question_left_in_a_wet_field
hopes words_that_need_no_return_address
topics animals +0.6 pastimes +0.4 food +0.4 gossip -0.3
styles comfort=wry gossip=oblique refuse=soft tease=dry
marks oath saying
persona #self.first# minds the cows by old sayings and turns a folded letter in a pocket when the sayings run out.

words
oath! = Hitch my heifer crooked | Butter that gatepost
saying! = Praise a hinge after shutting the gate | A cow listens hardest to the hand with the hay
gate = a wry little gate | a low field gate
knife = a keen pocket knife | a slim clean blade
shaft = a bent grass stem | a stiff straw
letter = a soft-edged fold | a worn packet
mudlip = the muddy margin | the soft bank
stool = a three-legged stool | a squat stool
barley = a spoon of barley | a few pale grains
air = the leaf-turning air | the sharp autumn air
heel = a neat heel turn | a little stamp
knot = a loop of string | a tight cross knot
ridge = the top of the pasture | the dry rise
row = the line of backs | the slow procession

any
opinion merchants mixed 1 : An asking price is a gate left open. See which beast wanders through.
opinion houses against 1 : The high pasture gets the light but the low cow still gives milk.
opinion dole for 2 : I'd rather fill a bowl than teach it a rhyme about waiting.
opinion road mixed 1 : The road's good enough when the gate at the far end opens to you.
opinion strangers mixed 1 : A quiet stranger costs less attention than a noisy friend. I can spare the attention.
opinion festivals for 1 : No cow was ever the worse for a herder going dancing on a free evening.

grown
line greet : Step through. I know how to mind a gate. | Room by the dry post.
line greet.return : A good welcome goes round like a pail. | You've found a comfortable ear for that.
line again.open : Back as quick as an unlatched gate.
line farewell : Take the rise before the mud takes you. | May your boots keep their first colour.
line farewell.return : And a gentle slope at your own finish. | A kind parting travels well without a rope.
line react.news : Let the news across one beast at a time. | I'll have the version with the mud on it.
line react.news.new : That's a calf of a story still.
line react.news.known : I know the tune to that one by its hooves.
line react.news.doubt : That account's got out through somebody's hedge.
line react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|loss|assault] : I'd hold the gate quite steady and let the grieving pass.
line react.news.glad [topic.kind=birth|marriage|work] : Worth a good dance outside when the work's through.
line ask.how : How many gates did today put in your way?
line answer.how : Standing on the dry tuft. Could do worse.
line sympathise : No saying will cure it. I can stay awhile.
line reassure : A muddy hoof needn't mean the whole way's lost.
line refuse : There's an earlier call on my quiet hour today.
line accept_decline : Your side of the hedge is yours to mind.
line preclose : I should put my own wandering thoughts away now.
line accept_preclose : Go make the last stretch a good one.
line ack : Yes. The hinge and the gatepost both.

scene exchange1
when topic=animals self.job=none self.workplace
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : At #self.workplace# I count backs above the grass. | I can move a cow without raising a stick.
b : Why not count heads? | How?
a : Heads stoop to eat. Backs stay honest. | Stand where the beast would rather not linger.

scene exchange2
when topic=food self.likes=barley_soup
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : The last grain of barley likes the edge of my spoon. | Barley plumps while I look away.
b : Do you leave it? | Won't it plump while watched?
a : No. A good spoon's got a patient owner. | It would. But the cows would be elsewhere.

scene exchange3
when topic=pastimes self.likes=dancing
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I practise a dance step against a gate. | I can dance in a narrow strip of dry grass.
b : Does the gate follow? | What about turns?
a : It yields quite handsomely if I lift the latch. | I turn my partner towards the mud only in imagination.

scene exchange4
when topic=work self.likes=sharp_knife
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A keen knife cuts a frayed end clean. | I test my blade on a loose hay stem.
b : Does a blunt one not cut? | Should it slice across?
a : It leaves three little jobs where there used to be one. | If it only flattens the straw I owe it some care.

scene exchange5
when topic=season self.likes=autumn_wind
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Autumn wind keeps count of all the leaves I've missed. | A leaf blowing uphill knows a short way home.
b : Are you counting leaves? | Does your herd follow?
a : I'm pretending to till the cattle make their minds up. | Cows prefer a longer way with something to eat along it.

scene exchange6
when topic=weather self.dislikes=deep_mud
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Deep mud talks like an old saying. | I look for a blade of grass standing tall in a mire.
b : What's it saying? | Is the ground firmer there?
a : Come early, stay late. I won't be quoting that one. | Sometimes. The grass has made its own lengthy study of that spot.

scene exchange7
when topic=home self.dislikes=wet_floors
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A wet floor teaches a dance nobody requested. | I wipe a pail bottom before I carry it inside.
b : A difficult figure to master? | Would a mat not do?
a : One foot out and supper over your head. Easily learnt. | A mat's a kind thing. My manners needn't depend on it.

scene exchange8
when topic=memories
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I keep one letter without another to go with it. | An old saying gives advice about the final word.
b : Was it lost? | Do you know it?
a : The letter's here. The reply never came. | I know where mine's folded. I'll let it rest.

scene exchange9
when topic=friends self.friend
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I'd save a favourite saying for #self.friend#. | I like the company of a wolf who can let a joke go by unherded.
b : Would a friend guess the end? | Does it come back?
a : A good one improves the ending behind my back. | Only if it was worth the walk.

scene exchange10
when topic=work self.job=none self.trade_words
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : The herd knows where the gate resists a muzzle. | A cow can eat while appearing to think deeply.
b : Have you mended the gap? | How does a herder distinguish them?
a : The gap knows another old saying. It's on its last hinge. | Try offering something greener.

scene exchange11
when topic=land here.region=ridgemere
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : From the headland the roofs look easy to herd. | I like a high patch for a rest.
b : Where would you drive them? | Watching over the city?
a : Up the hill. They're halfway persuaded already. | Watching a damp day get lost downhill.

scene exchange12
when topic=gossip here.gossip=yes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Word is #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. | Rumour can get through a narrower hedge than any cow.
b : Is there a saying for it? | Would you close the gap?
a : Yes, but I won't set it running across a second pasture. | I can close my own mouth without planting a thorn.

scene exchange13
when topic=times world.times=hard|lean
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : In lean weather I put a little more water to the barley. | A worn gate can be kept another season.
b : Still soup? | With what?
a : If it's stirred with purpose. | A sharp knife, a sound peg and the will to stop slamming it.

scene exchange14
when topic=dreams
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I mean to coin a saying that outwalked me. | I picture a reply arriving too late for the original question.
b : Have you one ready? | Would you open it?
a : Several. Their legs are still short. | I'd find a dry seat first.

scene exchange15
when topic=travel
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I walk the outside of a twisty lane. | I take a short stick to judge uncertain ground.
b : Isn't that farther? | How far do you push it?
a : More room to turn my feet when the tune catches me. | Till I find something less hopeful than the surface.

scene exchange16
when topic=faith
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I've heard a blessing that's shorter than the cow's tail giving the benediction. | I say a little grace over soup.
b : A cow in chapel? | Is it an old saying?
a : No. A herder can be grateful outside. | I'd be embarrassed to ask whether the Bright One remembers it.

scene exchange17
when topic=day
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I fold the letter once before going out. | When I come home I talk less than the cows do.
b : Does it need the crease? | What have they left to say?
a : I need to feel that the fold's still sound. | A whole day's worth of grass takes a while to digest.

scene exchange18
when topic=health
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : My shoulders swing easier after dancing. | I let the day's worry sit outside my collar.
b : Not too tired next morning? | Can you do that on purpose?
a : The feet sometimes send a different report. | Better since I learned which shoulder it favours.

story tale1 [topic=animals self.job=none]
  : A calf stood still for so long beside a gate I began explaining an old proverb about stubbornness. Very thoughtfully, I thought.
  : The calf slipped through as soon as I stopped leaning on the latch.
  b : What proverb fits that?
  : If a cow can't use the gate, inspect the wolf giving advice.

story tale2 [topic=memories]
  : I rewrote the beginning of a letter across a torn wrapper's reverse. The wind took the wrapper over a hedge.
  : I found it hanging on a thorn, turned to the side I'd meant to leave blank.
  b : Did you finish it there?
  : I put the scrap away. There wasn't a word I wanted hanging out to dry amongst the leaves.

story tale3 [topic=pastimes self.likes=dancing]
  : I once tried a new turn on a bit of hard ground where a pail had been. My whole right heel fitted the round print.
  : The turn was magnificent. Getting out of the print was a separate accomplishment.
  b : Did anybody see?
  : Several cows, admirably discreet. I taught myself the other foot on fresh ground.

rule @call1
when concept=bark working=yes self.job=none
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Slow backs moving. That's the colour of a good count. | A last look at the latch before I walk away.

rule @call2
when concept=bark here.time=dawn
does aside
cooldown 8h
say The grass looks ready for company. | A cold hinge likes lifting more than shoving.

rule @call3
when concept=bark here.weather=rain
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Another saying's gone ankle-deep in mud. | High path's keeping its own counsel today.

rule @call4
when concept=bark self.likes=barley_soup
does aside
cooldown 8h
say My spoon knows how deep the barley lies. | A good soup grows untidy round the edge.
dlg resident.hub : I'll trade you a plain answer for a question that stands still.
dlg resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : #topic.player#, same gate, a better opening this time.
dlg resident.hub [here.time=night] : An evening proverb tends to come with a yawn in the middle.
dlg resident.hub [pair.disposition=wary|hostile] : I can keep a civil hedge across the space between us.
dlg resident.job [self.job=none self.trade_words] : #self.trade_words.capitalize#. Whole days spent persuading grass to keep inside the gate. The grass has a walk about first, naturally.
dlg resident.job : I aim to be where my day's work is least expecting me.
dlg resident.news [world.news] : The bit that reached my pasture was #world.news#.
dlg resident.gossip [here.gossip=yes] : #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. There's a long fence between saying and seeing.
dlg resident.bye : Find your own dry tuft before evening.

graft resident.hub
choice How old does a saying have to be? -> new_saying
node new_saying
say Old enough for whoever said it first to have forgotten to ask payment. Then it belongs to every muddy boot that finds a use for it.
free
choice Did you make that one yourself? -> new_saying_again
choice Goodbye. -> bye
node new_saying_again
say I intend to forget your question. It'll sound a great deal wiser in a season.
choice Keep the gate swinging. -> hub
choice Goodbye. -> bye
